#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:151
msgid "Capabilities and Design Features"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:153
msgid "The system is capable of dosing chemicals for the following purposes. All wetted materials are compatible with the chemicals being dosed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:155
msgid "Disinfectant (normally sodium or calcium hypochlorite)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:157
msgid "Coagulant (typically polyaluminum chloride [PACl]). Use of another coagulant, such as alum, requires justification and bench-scale jar testing confirming that the proposed coagulant successfully forms flocs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:159
msgid "pH adjustment (if necessary)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:161
msgid "The chemical dosing system operates by gravity and does not depend on pumps or electrical power."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:163
msgid "Chemical dosing is flow-paced: the rate of chemical application automatically adjusts in proportion to the flow rate of water moving through the plant."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:165
msgid "The system is designed for easy disassembly and cleaning by the plant operator, including cleaning with vinegar to remove calcium carbonate deposits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:167
msgid "The plant operator adjusts the chemical dose (mass of chemical per volume of water passing through the plant) using the dose slider."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:171
msgid "Chemical Storage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:173
msgid "The plant includes at least two storage tanks for each chemical. Tank and fitting materials are compatible with the stored chemical. Compatible plastic storage tanks can be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:175
msgid "The combined storage volume for each chemical supplies the plant at maximum flow and maximum chemical dose for at least 48 hours."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:178
msgid "Chemical Feed System"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:180
msgid "The plant is equipped with a chemical feed system configured as shown in :numref:`figure_spec_PF_chemDoseController`. Materials that contact chemicals are compatible with each chemical and suitable for use with potable water."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:189
msgid "chemDoseController"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:189
msgid "Gravity-powered chemical feed system schematic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:191
#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:205
msgid "Constant Level Tank"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:193
msgid "From the chemical storage tanks, the chemical passes via gravity to a constant level tank (see :numref:`figure_spec_PF_constantLevelTank`). The chemical enters the constant level tank via a float valve, which maintains a constant level of chemical, providing a constant head to drive the chemical through the doser."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:195
msgid "The chemical level in the constant level tank is at the same elevation as the hanging weir when the slider is at the minimum position of the dosing lever."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:205
msgid "Constant level tank module for coagulant and chlorine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:207
msgid "Dosing Tubes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:209
msgid "Chemical flows from the constant level tank through resistors and then through tubes that terminate in a free discharge at the dose slider on the doser balance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:211
msgid "The resistors are sized to provide laminar flow over the desired range of chemical flow rates. Under laminar-flow conditions, the flow rate through the resistors is directly proportional to the elevation difference between the chemical level in the constant level tank and the dose slider."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:213
msgid "The plant includes a spare set of resistors for each chemical feed so that one set of tubes can be cleaned while the other set is in use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:215
msgid "Head loss through all other tubes and fittings connecting the constant level tank to the free discharge at the dose slider is less than 5% of the head loss through the resistors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:217
msgid "Chemical Dose Controller |Doser|"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:0
#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:229
msgid "Doser"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:219
msgid "One end of the chemical doser balance (see :numref:`figure_spec_PF_doser`) is connected to a float in the plant entrance tank. The dose slider and the ends of the doser hoses are located on the other end of the chemical doser balance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:229
msgid "Chemical dose controller designed for two independent chemical feeds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:232
msgid "Function"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:234
msgid "The doser balance is designed so that the operator can select a chemical dose (mass of chemical per volume of water) by moving the dose slider to a specific position along the lever. The lever, LFOM, and constant level tank then work together to adjust the chemical flow in proportion to the plant flow to maintain a constant chemical dose."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:236
msgid "When the plant flow is zero, the lever is horizontal and chemical flow is zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:238
msgid "When plant flow increases, the water level in the entrance tank increases (due to additional head loss through the LFOM), causing one end of the doser balance to rise. This, in turn, causes the other end of the balance, and the dose slider, to fall, increasing the elevation difference between the chemical level in the constant level tank and the dose slider. The greater driving head increases the chemical flow through the doser."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:240
msgid "The water level in the entrance tank is directly proportional to the plant flow rate (due to the LFOM). The dose slider elevation is directly proportional to the entrance tank water level. The chemical flow is directly proportional to the dose slider elevation. Therefore, the chemical flow is directly proportional to the plant flow, and changes in the slider position directly set the chemical dose."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:244
msgid "Flocculator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:246
msgid "For more detailed information, see :ref:`Flocculation Design <title_Flocculation_Design>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:249
#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:333
msgid "Design Basis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:251
msgid "The AguaClara flocculator is designed to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:253
msgid "Provide a head loss of 5 cm to provide gentle mixing and a residence time of approximately 1200 seconds (20 minutes), allowing aggregation of individual particles and small flocs into flocs large enough to settle out in the clarifier. This results in a velocity gradient of approximately 15 :math:`\\mathrm{s}^{-1}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:255
msgid "Facilitate manual sludge draining and maintenance by one person."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:258
msgid "Flow Paths"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:260
msgid "The flow passages are designed to be as close to square as possible given the constraints of the circular geometry and fabrication."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:262
msgid "The upstream water depth of the flocculator is determined by the depth required by the filter and is approximately 115 cm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:264
msgid "The spacing between baffles is designed to achieve the target head loss at the design flow rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:266
msgid "The ports between flocculator channels (see :numref:`figure_PFflocChannelPort`) have the same flow area as the space between the baffles so that each port improves flocculation without breaking flocs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:275
msgid "ports"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:275
msgid "Flocculator with side walls removed to show the port between channels. Water flows between flocculator channels through a port that has the same flow dimensions as the space between baffles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:278
msgid "Channel Construction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:280
msgid "The walls of the flocculation channels are vertical and maintain the channel width along both the length and height of each flocculator channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:282
msgid "The drain pipe is activated via a valve and is sized to empty the flocculator in less than 20 minutes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:284
msgid "Operation requires sufficient ambient lighting for the operator to observe floc formation. Operators need a flashlight to observe floc formation during power outages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:287
msgid "Baffles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:289
msgid "The flocculation baffles are removable. One operator working alone can raise a baffle module (see :numref:`figure_spec_PFbaffleModule`) so that water flows beneath the baffle and drains from the flocculator channel. Large flocculator configurations can have baffle modules that require more than one person for complete removal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:291
msgid "The flocculation baffles are constructed from stainless steel or polycarbonate sheets. The baffle-module frames are constructed from PVC and PEX pipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:301
msgid "Baffle Module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:301
msgid "The baffle modules transfer the force of the water to the downstream wall through the PVC and PEX pipe frame. Each flocculator channel holds one baffle module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:305
msgid "Clarifier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:307
msgid "For more detailed information, see :ref:`Clarifier Design <title_Clarifier_Design>`. The clarifier (see :numref:`figure_spec_PFclarifierElevation`) contains three separate processes: filtration in the floc filter, sedimentation in the plate settlers, and consolidation in the floc hopper. Flocculated water flows to the inlet manifold and then through the diffusers, jet reverser, floc filter, and plate settlers. Clarified water flows into the outlet manifold and out of the clarifier to the filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:309
msgid "The clarifier configuration is based on the coldest design water temperature and the lowest-density primary particles to be captured. Surface waters with high concentrations of dissolved organic matter and low concentrations of suspended solids produce low-density flocs; for these source waters, the design uses a lower velocity gradient in the inlet manifold and jet reverser. Some source-water conditions require a larger clarifier plan-view area to reduce the floc filter upflow velocity and plate settler capture velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:318
msgid "Clarifier elevation view"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:318
msgid "Elevation view of the clarifier with no wall, showing location of the floc filter, plate settlers, and floc hopper."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:320
msgid "The clarifier can be configured with multiple bays (see :numref:`figure_PF_clarifier_internals`) that work in parallel to treat the design flow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:330
msgid "Clarifier internals, showing two bays"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:330
msgid "Clarifier with two bays."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:335
msgid "The high-rate, vertical-flow clarifier is designed to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:337
msgid "To deliver flocs to the clarifier bay without breaking them into pieces with terminal velocities below the capture velocity of the plate settlers. This sets the maximum velocity gradient for the transfer of the flocs from the flocculator to the floc filter in the clarifier bay. The design limits the maximum velocity gradient to less than 360 :math:`\\mathrm{s}^{-1}`. Lower design values are used for raw waters with high concentrations of dissolved organic matter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:339
msgid "To provide a mechanism for the operator to dump poorly flocculated water before it enters the clarifier. This is important to reduce the recovery time when there is a flocculation failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:341
msgid "To produce a stable floc filter (fluidized suspension of flocs) that reduces the clarified water turbidity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:343
msgid "To provide evenly distributed low-velocity flow through the plate settlers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:345
msgid "To prevent accumulation of sludge that would tend to become anaerobic and release both dissolved organics (taste and odor issues) and methane bubbles that would carry flocs to the top of the clarifier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:347
msgid "To remove the solids without requiring power or moving mechanical parts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:349
msgid "To ensure easy operation and maintenance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:353
msgid "Inlet Manifold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:355
msgid "Flocculated water enters the inlet manifold and then exits through a series of diffusers in the bottom of the pipe. The end of the inlet manifold is capped. The minimum diameter of the inlet manifold is set by the velocity gradient downstream of the 90-degree elbow (see Equation :eq:`D_pipe_min_of_K_and_jet_G_max`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:358
msgid "Diffusers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:360
msgid "The diffusers point down to the bottom of the clarifier bay and extend along the length of the pipe at regular intervals to ensure that water is evenly distributed within the bay. Diffusers are designed to ensure that the jet exiting the jet reverser has a maximum velocity gradient that is less than the design constraint to prevent excessive floc breakup (see Equation :eq:`planejet_v_max_of_q`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:362
msgid "Diffusers are shaped so that one end is molded to a reduced diameter that fits into the inlet manifold port, and the other end is deformed to the shape of a rectangle (:numref:`figure_spec_PF_diffuser_dimensions`). This deformation is done to create a line jet entering the jet reverser in the bottom of the clarifier bay and to enhance flow distribution by maximizing the jet velocity given the constraints of Equation :eq:`planejet_v_max_of_q`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:371
msgid "Diffuser dimension definition"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:371
msgid "Dimensions and geometry of a diffuser. The image on the left shows a view of a diffuser from the end of a clarifier bay. The image on the right shows a view of a diffuser from the side of a clarifier bay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:374
msgid "Jet Reverser"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:376
msgid "The jet reverser consists of a longitudinally cut half-pipe that is laid in the bottom of the bay (see :numref:`figure_spec_PF_clarifierEndView`). It keeps flocs suspended in the clarifier by ensuring that the force of the diffuser jet propels settled flocs back upward."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:378
msgid "The diffusers are offset from the jet reverser centerline. This is intentionally done to promote the resuspension of flocs, which form a floc filter for primary filtration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:387
msgid "Clarifier showing jet reverser"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:387
msgid "End view of a clarifier bay showing the sloped bottom, inlet manifold, diffusers, and jet reverser. The diffusers direct a jet of water into one side of the jet reverser."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:390
msgid "Floc Filter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:392
msgid "Floc filters significantly improve the performance of a clarifier and reduce the clarified water turbidity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:394
msgid "The line jet from the diffusers enters the jet reverser to force flow up through the clarifier bay. The vertical upward jet momentum is used to resuspend flocs that have settled to the bottom of the clarifier bay. The resuspended flocs form a fluidized bed, which is a floc filter. Small particles are captured by the flocs in the floc filter as the small particles flow through the flocs. The bed is fluidized because flocs are kept in suspension by the upflowing water."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:396
msgid "Clarifiers use an upflow velocity of 1 mm/s in the floc filter. This velocity is measured above the sloped bottom in the section of the clarifier bay with vertical walls."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:398
msgid "For a floc filter to form, a clarifier requires that:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:400
msgid "The plate settlers capture small flocs and cause them to aggregate into larger flocs as they avalanche back into the floc filter zone. The increased terminal velocity of the larger flocs enables them to create a stable floc filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:402
msgid "All settled flocs are resuspended by the vertical jet of water exiting the jet reverser."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:405
msgid "Sloped Bottom Geometry"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:407
msgid "The clarifier bottom geometry (see :numref:`figure_spec_PF_clarifierEndView`) prevents sludge accumulation while also ensuring good flow distribution. The slope on either side of the diffusers is at a 60-degree angle above horizontal. The bottom geometry allows for smooth flow expansion to the entire plan-view area of the bay and ensures that all flocs that settle are transported to the jet reverser. The diffusers do not touch the bottom of the tank so that flocs on both sides of the diffuser can return to the jet reverser for resuspension. Thus, there is no accumulation of settled flocs in the main clarifier bays."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:410
#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:423
msgid "Floc Hopper"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:412
msgid "The floc hopper (:numref:`figure_spec_PFflocHopper`) provides an opportunity for floc consolidation. The floc weir controls the depth of the floc filter because as the floc filter grows, it will eventually reach the top of the floc weir. Because flocs are denser than water, the flocs “spill” over the edge of the floc weir, which allows the floc filter to stay at a constant height while sludge accumulates and consolidates in the floc hopper. Operators can use a flashlight to observe the floc filter moving into the lower end of the plate settlers, indicating that the floc hopper is full. The sludge is drained from the floc hopper by opening a valve or by lowering the end of a flexible tube. The operator can observe that the sludge has completely drained from the floc hopper by the clarity of the water exiting from the floc hopper drain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:414
msgid "The floc hopper allows for a self-cleaning clarifier. Operators only have to clean the clarifier once every three to six months because there is no stagnant accumulation of anoxic sludge."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:423
msgid "Flocs enter the hopper by flowing over the floc weir. Sludge is drained from the bottom of the hopper."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:426
msgid "Plate Settlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:428
msgid "After flowing through the floc filter, flocs reach the plate settlers. Plate settlers are sloped surfaces that provide additional settling area for flocs, thereby increasing the effective settling area of the clarifier without increasing the plan view area. AguaClara plate settlers are sloped at 50 degrees. The spacing between plates is 1.5 cm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:430
msgid "The plate settlers are made from clear polycarbonate sheets or stainless steel. The sheets are assembled in modules (see :numref:`figure_spec_PFplateSettlerModule`). The modules are light enough to be removed from the clarifier by hand. A PVC pipe frame attached to the clarifier walls supports the plate settler modules."
msgstr ""

